 ## Overview
-SDF Smooth Union is a method to combine two or more Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) into a single smooth SDF, avoiding sharp edges at the intersection of primitives [S2, S3, unofficial]. It approximates the boolean union operation using a blending parameter $k$ [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings, such as [Multi-Material Distance Fields](1943-multi-material-distance-fields.md), by focusing on geometric blending of distances rather than material interpolation, although it can be extended to blend materials [S3, unofficial].
+SDF Smooth Union is a method to combine two or more Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) into a single smooth SDF, avoiding sharp edges at the intersection of primitives [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It approximates the boolean union operation using a blending parameter $k$ [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings, such as [Multi-Material Distance Fields](1943-multi-material-distance-fields.md), by focusing on geometric blending of distances rather than material interpolation, although it can be extended to blend materials [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method combines individual SDF results to produce a unified distance field. Two primary formulations exist: the polynomial smooth minimum (often called `smin`) and the LogSumExp smooth minimum.
